---
import { LINKS } from '../config/site';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const data = [
  {
    data: 'Mail fetched from your account',
    where: 'Memory, inside the enclave',
    kept: 'Not stored',
  },
  {
    data: 'Mail received by forwarding',
    where: 'Encrypted storage, under your key',
    kept: 'Until delivered, or a short limit',
  },
  {
    data: 'Tokens for your mailbox',
    where: 'Encrypted database, under your key',
    kept: 'Until you disconnect',
  },
  {
    data: 'Rules and access settings',
    where: 'Encrypted database, under your key',
    kept: 'Until you delete them',
  },
  {
    data: 'Receipts',
    where: 'Encrypted database, hash chained',
    kept: 'You decide, with a default limit',
  },
  {
    data: 'Your account email and billing details',
    where: 'Our database and our payment provider',
    kept: 'Until you delete your account',
  },
  {
    data: 'Devices that signed in to your account',
    where: 'Our database, as a hash of a random cookie',
    kept: 'A year from each one’s last sign-in, 50 at most',
  },
];
---

<Base title="Privacy" description="What we keep, and why.">
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <nav class="tabs" aria-label="Legal">
        <a href="/privacy/" aria-current="page">Privacy</a>
        <a href={LINKS.terms}>Terms</a>
        <a href={LINKS.dataProcessing}>Data processing</a>
        <a href={LINKS.subprocessors}>Subprocessors</a>
      </nav>
      <p class="small updated">Updated 12 November 2026</p>
      <h1 class="h-l title">What we keep, and why</h1>
      <p class="intro plain">This is the plain version of our privacy policy. We don’t sell, mine or advertise on email, and we don’t train models on it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-end">
    <div class="wrap stack sections">
      <div class="tbl-scroll">
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Data</th>
              <th scope="col">Where it lives</th>
              <th scope="col">How long</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              data.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{row.data}</th>
                  <td>{row.where}</td>
                  <td>{row.kept}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What we do with your mail</h2>
        <p>The service reads mail inside the enclave to apply your rules, then releases only what you approved to the connection you approved. No third-party model reads it, and it isn’t used to train anything.</p>
        <h2>Who else is involved</h2>
        <p>Your mail provider hosts your mail, and we can’t change that. AWS runs the hardware. Connections you approve receive what you approved, and what they do next is covered by their own policies.</p>
        <h2>What we can see and do with your account</h2>
        <p>We see your account’s contact address, name, the ways you sign in and how many sessions you have, never your mail. Through the service we can block your account, which signs every device out and lets nobody sign in, and unblock it. We can ask to delete it, which takes the same seven days as when you ask and which you can cancel unless it’s also blocked, and we can cancel a deletion. We can sign all your devices out, and revoke invitations nobody has used yet.</p>
        <p>We can’t enter your account, read your mail, change your contact address, or add or remove a way you sign in. So if you lose your email, your passkeys and your recovery codes, support can’t give your account back. The service counts each kind of these actions since it last started and publishes the counts at <code>/status</code>, never naming an account.</p>
        <h2>Mail we send you</h2>
        <p>We send sign-in codes and links, and a code to the new address when you change your contact address. We also tell you about security and access, and you can’t turn these off: a sign-in from a device that hadn’t signed in to your account before, a change in how you sign in (passkeys, signing in with another account, passkey only, recovery codes), a change of your contact address, sent to the old address and the new one, a deletion asked for or cancelled, and your account blocked or unblocked. A notice holds no code or link. AWS carries this mail to your mail provider.</p>
        <h2>Cookies</h2>
        <p>The app sets three cookies, for signing in and for your account’s security, and none for analytics or advertising. The session cookie keeps you signed in, for seven days without use and thirty at most. A short-lived cookie ties a sign-in with Google, GitHub, Meta or Microsoft to the browser that started it. The device cookie is a random value, kept for a year from your last sign-in, whose only use is to tell you by mail when a device that hadn’t signed in to your account before does. Your account keeps a hash of it, for up to 50 devices. This site sets no cookies.</p>
        <h2>Your rights</h2>
        <p>You can export your data, revoke any access and delete your account at any time, on any plan. Mail a connection already received can’t be recalled, and we show you what each one holds, so you can ask them to delete it.</p>
        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>Write to <a class="link" href="mailto:privacy@permaily.com">privacy@permaily.com</a>. If you’re in the EU or UK, you can also complain to your local data protection authority.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .tabs {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-24);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-40);
    border-bottom: var(--hair) solid var(--rule);
  }

  .tabs a {
    margin-bottom: -1px;
    padding-block: var(--space-10);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: var(--text-body);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .tabs a[aria-current='page'] {
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .updated {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-12);
  }

  .title {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-20);
  }

  .plain {
    max-width: 38em;
  }

  .sections {
    gap: var(--space-48);
  }

  .prose {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-24);
    max-width: 42em;
  }

  .prose h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-heading);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-h);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  .prose p {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
</style>
